1. Secure an AWS Elastic IP
Go to EC2 Console > Elastic IPs > Allocate.
Choose Actions > Associate to attach it permanently to your running instance to avoid IP changes.

2. Generate a DDNS Key on No-IP
Log into No-IP with your Google account.
Go to Dynamic DNS > DDNS Keys (or DNS Records), 
click Generate DDNS Key, and copy the unique Username and Password provided.

3. Install and Configure ddclient on Ubuntu
Connect to your EC2 instance via SSH and run:
sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y ddclient

Open the config file (sudo nano /etc/ddclient.conf), clear it, and paste this block using
your DDNS key details:

ssl=yes
protocol=noip
use=web, web=ipify-ipv4
server=://no-ip.com
login=YOUR_DDNS_KEY_USERNAME
password=YOUR_DDNS_KEY_PASSWORD
mytestsever.myftp.biz

4. Enable and Start the ServiceConfigure the client to run continuously in the background and start it:
sudo sed -i 's/run_daemon="false"/run_daemon="true"/' /etc/default/ddclient
sudo systemctl restart ddclient && sudo systemctl enable ddclient

